## Implementation Strategy

### Phase 1: Core Scanner (Week 1)
- Recursive directory scanning
- File metadata collection
- Type detection (by extension)

### Phase 2: Database Layer (Week 1-2)
- SQLite schema setup
- Insert/query operations
- Database initialization

### Phase 3: Search Engine (Week 2)
- Full-text search on metadata
- Filter by file type, date, size
- Sorting and limiting results

### Phase 4: Export & CLI (Week 2-3)
- JSON export of results
- Command-line interface
- Basic user workflows

---

## Key Design Decisions

**Why SQLite?**
- No external server required
- Supports complex queries for later versions
- Fast for local file indexing
- Standard Python library (sqlite3)

**Why JSON for metadata?**
- Human-readable
- Easy to extend without schema changes
- Compatible with Ollama later
- Simple to export/backup

**Why Python?**
- Your preference (TigerMemory Dev modelfile)
- Excellent file handling (pathlib)
- Strong database support
- Great for beginner-friendly code

---

## Constraints & Considerations

**Windows Laptop Performance**:
- Optimize for slower disk I/O
- Use batch database inserts
- Avoid heavy memory operations during scanning

**Privacy & Safety**:
- Never upload file contents
- Only store metadata locally
- Respect .gitignore-like patterns
- Option to exclude sensitive folders

**Beginner-Friendly**:
- Clear error messages
- Simple CLI (no complex flags)
- Good inline code comments
- Step-by-step documentation

---

